.TH EUCLID-CLI-EKM-IMPORT-CERTIFICATE 1 "September 2026" "euclid-cli 1.0.53" "Euclid CLI Manual"
.SH NAME
euclid-cli-ekm-import-certificate \- store a certificate and its private key
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B euclid-cli ekm import-certificate
.BR \-\-name | \-n
.I NAME
.BR \-\-certificate | \-f
.I FILE
.BR \-\-key | \-k
.I FILE
[\fB\-\-description\fR|\fB\-d\fR \fITEXT\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
Stores a certificate somebody else issued, together with its private
key, under a name.
.PP
An API gateway listener configured with
.RI \(dq protocol \(dq: \(dq https \(dq
serves the certificate its
.RI \(dq certificate \(dq
setting names, so importing under that name is how a real certificate
replaces the self-signed one euclid generated for it. It is also how a
renewed certificate is rolled out: importing again under the same name
replaces what is stored there, and the listener picks it up when it
next starts.
.PP
The private key is checked against the certificate before either is
stored. A mismatched pair would otherwise be accepted here and only
show itself later as a handshake that fails for every caller. Once
stored, the key never leaves the server: no action reports it, and
.BR euclid-cli-ekm-get-certificate (1)
returns everything but.
.PP
The certificate file may hold a chain, leaf first with its
intermediates after it, exactly as a certificate authority issues one.
The whole chain is stored and served, which is what lets a client build
a path to a trust anchor it already has.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-n ", " \-\-name " " \fINAME\fR
Name the certificate is stored under, and the one a listener's
.RI \(dq certificate \(dq
setting names. Required.
.TP
.BR \-f ", " \-\-certificate " " \fIFILE\fR
Path to the PEM certificate file. Required.
.TP
.BR \-k ", " \-\-key " " \fIFILE\fR
Path to the PEM private key file, unencrypted. Required.
.TP
.BR \-d ", " \-\-description " " \fITEXT\fR
What the certificate is for. Free text, never interpreted. Optional.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The certificate was stored; its name, subject, issuer, fingerprint and
validity period are printed as JSON.
.TP
.B 1
A file could not be read, the certificate is not a PEM-encoded X.509
certificate, the key does not belong to it, the server could not be
reached, or it rejected the request.
.SH EXAMPLES
.RS
.nf
euclid-cli --endpoint https://localhost:5566 ekm import-certificate \e
    --name eag-production \e
    --certificate /etc/ssl/certs/api.example.com.pem \e
    --key /etc/ssl/private/api.example.com.key \e
    --description "api.example.com, renewed quarterly"
.fi
.RE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR euclid-cli-ekm (1),
.BR euclid-cli-ekm-create-certificate (1),
.BR euclid-cli-ekm-list-certificates (1),
.BR euclid-cli-ekm-get-certificate (1),
.BR euclid-cli-ekm-delete-certificate (1),
.BR euclid-cli-eag (1)
